<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Gazebo wrapper around drone.dae. drone.urdf next to it is the RViz-side
  consumer of the same mesh and is left untouched; this file is the gz-sim one.

  MESH FRAME

  The SolidWorks export is referenced to a bbox corner, not a real origin:
  raw bounds run 0 -> +extent on all three axes, and the file is tagged Y_UP.
    bbox min    = [0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0001]
    bbox max    = [0.4733, 0.1314, 0.4744]   (mesh axes, Y is up)
    bbox centre = [0.2367, 0.0657, 0.2373]
  There is no <unit meter="..."> in the .dae, so gz reads it as metres - the
  0.47 m span across the arms is already correct and <scale> stays 1 1 1.

  UP-AXIS AND THE VISUAL POSE

  gz-common's ColladaLoader honours <up_axis>, so it applies the Y_UP -> Z_UP
  rotation (+90 deg about X) itself before gz-sim ever sees the vertices. The
  pose below therefore only has to do what is left over from drone.urdf's
  rpy="1.5708 0 -1.5708": the -90 deg yaw, plus the translation that pulls the
  corner-referenced mesh back onto base_link.

  >>> IF THE DRONE RENDERS STANDING ON ITS EDGE, the loader did NOT convert
  >>> Y_UP. Fix by rolling it here - change the visual pose rpy to
  >>>     1.5708 0 -1.5708
  >>> and leave xyz alone; the translation is identical either way. This is the
  >>> same failure mode, and the same fix, that drone.urdf documents for RViz.

  base_link sits where drone.urdf puts it, so Gazebo and RViz agree: 0.037 m
  aft of the mesh's geometric centre in x, centred in y, and level with the
  underside of the frame in z (mesh bottom at z=0). That x offset is inherited
  deliberately - do not "correct" it to 0.2372 without also changing the URDF,
  or the bag-replay view and the sim view will stop matching.

  NO MATERIAL TAG, ON PURPOSE

  prep_mesh.py baked face colours into the .dae. A <material> here would
  override them and render the airframe flat grey - same reason drone.urdf
  omits one on its visual.

  WHAT THIS MODEL IS NOT

  There are no rotor links, joints, MulticopterMotorModel plugins or sensors,
  so this cannot fly and is not a PX4 airframe yet. The inertial is a box
  inertia over the bounding box at a guessed 1.5 kg mass - a placeholder to
  keep the physics solver happy while the model rests on the ground, NOT a
  measured value. Replace it before this ever backs an airframe file.
  For a purely visual spawn (no falling, no contacts), set <static>true</static>.
-->
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <model name='indoor_dev_drone'>
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <static>false</static>

    <link name='base_link'>
      <!-- Placeholder. Pose is the mesh bbox centre expressed in base_link. -->
      <inertial>
        <pose>-0.0373 0.0005 0.0657 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>1.5</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.030151</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.030288</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.056119</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <gravity>true</gravity>
      <velocity_decay />

      <visual name='base_link_visual'>
        <pose>0.2 0.2372 0 1.5708 0 -1.5708</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <scale>1 1 1</scale>
            <uri>model://indoor_dev_drone/drone.dae</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <!-- Bounding box of the mesh, not a hull: 13 MB of triangles is far too
           much to hand the collision engine, and the arms are open frame so a
           convex hull would be a box anyway. Extents are the measured bbox
           mapped through the visual rotation (mesh z -> x, mesh x -> y,
           mesh y -> z), centred on the same bbox centre as the inertial. -->
      <collision name='base_link_collision'>
        <pose>-0.0373 0.0005 0.0657 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.4744 0.4733 0.1315</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>1.0</mu>
              <mu2>1.0</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
